$[K[?25h]0;◐ Lighthouse sentences list]0;◑ Lighthouse sentences list]0;◐ Lighthouse sentences list]0;◑ Lighthouse sentences list]0;◐ Lighthouse sentences list]0;◑ Lighthouse sentences list[?25l[6;10H   ( running Stop hook )[9;2H[?25h]133;C]133;D[?25l[6;1Hclaude: 1. Lighthouses guide ships safely past dangerous coastlines.[K
2. Their beams can be seen for dozens of kilometers on a clear night.[K
3. Many early lighthouses burned wood or coal in open fires at the top.[K
4. The Pharos of Alexandria was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.[K
5. Modern lighthouses are usually powered by electricity and run automatically.
6. Each lighthouse has a distinctive light pattern so sailors can identify it.
7. Some flash in groups, while others flash at regular intervals.
8. Fog signals, such as horns or bells, warn ships when visibility is poor.
9. Lightkeepers once tended the lamps, polished the lenses, and kept logs.
10. The Fresnel lens, invented in 1822, dramatically increased lighthouse brightness.
11. Its prisms focus light into a powerful, narrow beam.
12. Many lighthouses are painted in bold stripes for daytime recognition.
13. Some lighthouses stand on rocky islands far from any mainland.
14. Others rise from the shoreline or sit atop tall cliffs.
15. Lighthouse towers are often built of stone, brick, or cast iron.
16. Storms have destroyed many lighthouses over the centuries.
17. Engineers designed some towers to withstand crashing waves and hurricane winds.
18. Lighthouses have inspired countless paintings, poems, and photographs.
19. Keepers' families often lived in cottages beside the tower.
20. Automation in the twentieth century made most keepers unnecessary.
21. Many historic lighthouses are now preserved as museums or tourist attractions.
22. Some have been converted into private homes or bed-and-breakfast inns.
23. Lighthouse beacons use lamps that rotate to sweep their light across the sea.
24. The rotation speed helps mariners tell one lighthouse from another.
25. Lighthouse lenses are often made of finely cut glass.
26. Some coastal lights mark the entrances to harbors and channels.
27. Others warn of submerged reefs, sandbars, or shipwreck sites.
28. Ships' navigators rely on lighthouse charts printed with each light's characteristics.
29. Radio beacons and GPS have reduced, but not eliminated, the need for lighthouses.
30. Many lighthouses are still active aids to navigation today.
31. Some lighthouses carry a foghorn that sounds in a set pattern.
32. Lighthouse keepers sometimes faced months of isolation at remote stations.
33. Lamps once needed constant fuel, wicks, and careful maintenance.
34. Lighthouses are often sited at points where shipping routes converge.
35. Their stone walls can stand for more than two centuries.
36. Lighthouses mark the boundary between danger and safe passage.
37. Night-time beams have guided fishing boats home for generations.
38. Lighthouse tenders once resupplied stations by boat.
39. The tallest lighthouses rise more than one hundred meters above sea level.
40. Lighthouses remain enduring symbols of hope, vigilance, and the sea.
